WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.330
Donald Trump has a very nationalistic overall
worldview

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:06.880
that carries over into his trade policy.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.420
“...remember that! America first! America first!"

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:15.020
He sees these kinds of things as very much zero-sum: in which one side wins and another side loses.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.020
“We’ve lost our jobs like we’re
a bunch of babies.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.480
They’ve gone to other countries.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:20.490
They’ve gone to Mexico.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.580
They’ve gone to many other countries.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.470
...It’s all so easy, believe me.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.570
It’s all so easy.”

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:33.100
But in a complex system like global trade,
it’s not always easy to tell who is winning.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:39.420
Trump has suggested really really really strongly
that he wants to crack down on imports from Mexico.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.159
“...and plants and factories and everything

00:00:41.159 --> 00:00:43.039
else going into Mexico.”

00:00:43.039 --> 00:00:47.579
...and that has driven down the value of Mexico’s
currency an enormous amount.

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:52.379
Ironically, making the peso cheaper makes
it an even more attractive place to locate

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:56.659
production because it means that, in effect,
Mexican workers are being paid less

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:01.179
and makes it harder for American factories to compete
with Mexican ones.

00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:03.989
But that’s not what Donald Trump says is
going to happen.

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:08.760
“...but here's what happens: I will tell them, ‘You’re gonna move back, right?’

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.640
and they are going to say ‘Yes, sir. We’re moving back to the United States.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.420
We’re going to build our factory in the
United States.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.720
We’re not moving to Mexico and
we are going to create a lot of new employment!'"

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.680
Trump blames the loss of manufacturing jobs on trade deals like NAFTA:

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.440
The North American Free Trade Agreement

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.920
that reduced barriers to trade between The US, Canada, and Mexico.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.520
"NAFTA will tear down trade barriers between our three nations.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:37.960
It will create the world's largest trade zone

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.140
and create 200,00 jobs in this country."

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:42.860
"We're not getting anything.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:46.840
We have NAFTA, which is a total and complete disaster."

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.240
While trade deals are an easy target on the
campaign trail,

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.100
they are only one part of the equation.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:58.840
When you look at the really long term, you
can see that manufacturing jobs have been

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:06.520
declining as a share of the economy very steadily
for a very long time

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:12.260
and that tells us that, even though trade
deals matter, they are not the driver of this big trend.

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:18.120
It’s just, over the long haul, &nbsp;because of automation, as
economies evolve and advance,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.220
more people work in the service sector, fewer people work in the manufacturing sector.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.600
Trade plays a role in that, but it’s not
the primary driver.

00:02:25.609 --> 00:02:30.819
Despite this fact, it’s exactly those jobs
that Trump is claiming to protect by withdrawing

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:33.409
from the TPP: The Trans-Pacific Partnership.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.240
“A great thing for the American worker, what we just did.”

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.280
This particular trade deal, it was designed

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:46.290
to take a lot of Asian countries and bind
them more closely together with The United States;

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:55.240
one reason countries were eager to do a trade deal with The United States, rather than with China,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.620
is that they are closer to China and thus they are a little bit afraid of China

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.220
and they want to keep The United States invested in Asia.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:07.140
If we pull out, that may leave some of these countries feeling exposed or alone

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.040
and like they need to enter into a Chinese economic orbit.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.531
This decision means fewer cheap imports,

00:03:13.531 --> 00:03:16.951
but it also affects the market for American exports.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:21.560
One thing that's worth noting here is that
farmers would've been big winners under

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:23.060
Trans Pacific Partnership.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:27.219
In particular, I think the expectation was
that the United States would be able

00:03:27.219 --> 00:03:31.329
to export a lot more agricultural goods to
Japan.

00:03:31.329 --> 00:03:36.289
If The US has joined the TPP, American farmers would have had a stronger relationship with Japan,

00:03:36.289 --> 00:03:39.340
the world’s fourth largest agricultural consumer.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:43.920
Rural voters broke very very heavily in favor
of Donald Trump,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:50.800
but they may actually lose out by his sort of less trade-friendly policies.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.939
And it just shows that the world is complicated
and it’s difficult to change things.

00:03:55.939 --> 00:04:00.040
And, you really do need to talk to expert
people.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:05.079
“I am a free trader one hundred percent, but we need smart people making the deals

00:04:05.079 --> 00:04:07.569
and we don’t have smart people making
the deals.”

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:11.920
Trump’s view is that for us to win at trade
somebody else has to be losing.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.540
"This country is going to start winning again.

00:04:14.549 --> 00:04:17.609
We don't win anymore."

00:04:17.609 --> 00:04:20.000
I don't think that that is true.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.680
If he proves me wrong, you know, I think he’ll
have a great deal to brag about, but I think

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:29.140
he’s going to find that, you know, the forces
dragging American manufacturing employment

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:33.760
down are fairly profound and that the kinds
of things he thinks will fix it

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.540
are just as likely to make things worse as better.

